The present paper adds new findings to its first part [ 25 ], providing insight into the tensile and fatigue behavior, as well as the microstructural characteristics, of aged 18Ni300M steel processed through PBF-LB using gas-atomized (GA) and water-atomized (WA) powders, in comparison to the conventional VIM/VAR material. The main conclusions drawn from this investigation are: Microstructural analysis indicated that while GA and WA batches exhibited similar solidification cells, the WA batch contained more discontinuities, such as secondary phase particles, which contributed to its inferior mechanical performance. XEDS mapping confirmed these inclusions were oxides rich in aluminum, silicon, and titanium. In contrast, the GA samples also showed oxides when analyzed by TEM, but at the nanometric scale, depicting core–shell structures composed of Ti, Al, and O. The engineering stress–strain curves demonstrated indirectly that the aging heat treatment enhanced the mechanical strength of the GA and VIM/VAR samples while reducing the ductility. In contrast, the tensile properties of the aged WA specimens were severely compromised, leading to premature failure. Although the critical defect size estimated by LEFM theory is larger than the dimensions of the defects found in the fracture surface of a representative tensile aged WA specimen, both are in the same order of magnitude (10 1  μm). Furthermore, due to the high surface fraction occupied by these discontinuities ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
